Subject: base: per-field fingerprint overrides on the command line

A seed gives a coherent device but not a chosen one. An operator who needs a
specific timezone to match a proxy exit, or a specific renderer string because
that is what their target expects, has had no way to say so short of writing a
whole profile by hand and passing --apostate-profile. These eight switches are
that way: each replaces exactly the field it names, and the seed still fills
everything else, so the result is one device with a correction rather than two
partial sources of identity.

  --fingerprint-gpu-vendor              WebGL UNMASKED_VENDOR_WEBGL
  --fingerprint-gpu-renderer            WebGL UNMASKED_RENDERER_WEBGL
  --fingerprint-hardware-concurrency    navigator.hardwareConcurrency
  --fingerprint-device-memory           installed memory, in GiB
  --fingerprint-screen-width            screen.width
  --fingerprint-screen-height           screen.height
  --fingerprint-timezone                the IANA timezone name
  --fingerprint-locale                  the Accept-Language list

An override restricts the draw where it can and lands on the composed profile
where it cannot, and which of the two it is determines whether the result stays
coherent.

The GPU identity is the case that has to restrict. Vendor and renderer are not
free strings: the GL limits, the extension list, the shader precisions and the
WebGPU adapter all come from the anchor those strings were measured on, so a
renderer written over a finished profile sits on a capability table belonging to
different silicon. HonestyGaps measured the size of that mismatch through the
shipped binary: a backend-matched anchor delivers exactly what it claims, 39 of
39 WebGL1 and 36 of 36 WebGL2 extensions, while a cross-backend anchor is short
by OVR_multiview2. One name is enough. So --fingerprint-gpu-renderer narrows the
gpu_identity option set before the draw, the chosen option is still a measured
member of the resolved anchor, and WebGPU still comes from that member. A name
the resolved anchor never measured is refused, with the servable identities
listed, because emitting it would be a weaker position than the real renderer.

The scalar fields land on the profile, after the axis loop. Cores and memory are
refused above host capability, for the reason capacity has always been reduced
and never raised: a page can measure parallel throughput and can allocate until
it fails, so a claim above the machine is falsifiable while a claim below it is
not. The same reduction that backs the drawn values is used as the predicate, so
there is one rule and one place it lives. Memory is taken in GiB because that is
the unit navigator.deviceMemory reports in; the profile field is real installed
bytes, which Chromium rounds to the nearer power of two itself, and after patch
0087 that figure also sizes V8's heap, so performance.memory.jsHeapSizeLimit
agrees with the claim instead of contradicting it.

A screen override is applied before the available rectangle is derived, so
availWidth, availHeight, availLeft and availTop follow from the same desktop
furniture the seed drew. It is refused when it is smaller than a --window-size
the launch also asked for, and when the drawn furniture insets do not fit inside
it. Neither the host's real display size nor its real work area is knowable from
base/ at composition time -- there is no display yet -- so --window-size is the
only host bound available here, and that is a residual rather than a check.

The locale overrides are applied where the locale policy resolves rather than
over the finished profile, because the speech-voice table is keyed on the
resolved Accept-Language list. Applied later, a launch would claim en-GB while
offering the voice set measured for whatever the seed drew. The keyboard layout
map stays as the policy set it, which is correct rather than convenient: all
four catalogue locale policies carry the same map today, and a locale that does
not match the physical layout is an ordinary thing on a real machine.

Host pass-through interacts with all of this, and the interaction is settled
here. The reserved --fingerprint spellings -- host, off, false, 0, disable,
disabled, case-insensitively -- are decided in seed.cc, which patch 0081 owns;
this patch adds no host-value handling of its own and its diff touches only
compose.h and compose.cc. What it adds is the consequence: under any of those
spellings nothing is composed, so there is no profile for a persona, a pinned
anchor or a per-field override to land on, and combining them refuses the
launch instead of half-applying. Refusing rather than ignoring is the whole
point of this patch. An operator who mistypes a switch and is not told runs an
entire session believing a field was spoofed when it was their own hardware,
and that is indistinguishable from success until a site has already clustered
the real device. --fingerprint-explain still works with host mode, which is why
it is not in the refused set.

Every refusal writes to stderr and exits non-zero. An override that is honoured
but is not one of the catalogue's buckets is honoured and reported: the explain
report names the field, the value, the command line as the layer that decided
it, and the drawn value it displaced, and says plainly that a hand-chosen value
has no prevalence data behind it and may be more distinctive than a drawn one.

No new page-visible observable. A page cannot read a command line, and every one
of these switches writes a value into a field the profile already carried, of a
kind the catalogue could already have drawn. With no override switch present
nothing about a launch changes at all: the switches are absent, the draws are
the same draws, and the composed profile is byte-identical to the one the same
seed produced before this patch.

